WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:144533

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.900
This lesson introduces the
concept of conversable agent, a building

2
00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.900
agent class Autogen that can be used
to construct multi-agent conversations.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.933
You will learn
about the basic functionality,

4
00:00:13.933 --> 00:00:17.133
as well as build your first two agent chat

5
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:21.733
and show a fun conversation
between two stand up comedies.

6
00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:25.600
Let's dive in.

7
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.600
Let's begin
with learning the concept of an agent.

8
00:00:29.033 --> 00:00:32.033
In AutoGen, an agent is an entity

9
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.800
that can act on behalf of human intent,

10
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.700
send messages,
receive messages, perform actions,

11
00:00:39.033 --> 00:00:42.033
generate replies, and interact
with other agents.

12
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.100
AutoGen has a built-in agent
class called "conversable agent".

13
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:51.000
it unifies different types of agents
in the same programming abstraction.

14
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.233
It comes with a lot of built
in, functionalities.

15
00:00:54.233 --> 00:00:58.600
For example, you can use a list of LLM
configurations to generate replies,

16
00:00:58.833 --> 00:01:02.200
or we can do code execution or function
and tool execution.

17
00:01:02.433 --> 00:01:03.733
It also provides a component

18
00:01:03.733 --> 00:01:07.433
for keeping human in the loop
and checking for stopping the response.

19
00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:11.300
You can switch each component on and off
and customize it

20
00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:15.133
to suit the needs of your application.
Using these different capabilities

21
00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:19.100
You can create agents of different roles
using the same interface.

22
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.900
In the beginning, let's import the

23
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
OpenAI API key from the environment.

24
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:34.433
So we will import the get_openai_api_key

25
00:01:34.433 --> 00:01:39.100
utility function,
run it to get the OpenAI API key.

26
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.133
And then define a LLM configuration.

27
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.900
In this course
we will use GPT-3.5 turbo as a model.

28
00:01:48.333 --> 00:01:52.800
And next, let's import
the conversable agent class from AutoGen.

29
00:01:54.133 --> 00:01:56.400
And we will create our first conversable

30
00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.400
agent object.

31
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.600
Here we use, conversable agent

32
00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.333
class to define an agent named Chatbot.

33
00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:10.200
We pass this LLM configuration
as we defined up there

34
00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.300
to this conversable agent.

35
00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:17.133
So then this agent will be able to use
that large language model to generate the reply.

36
00:02:17.333 --> 00:02:20.900
And we also define
the human input mode as "never".

37
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:25.300
This means the agent will never,
seek for human input.

38
00:02:25.733 --> 00:02:28.733
It will only use this large language model
to generate a reply.

39
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:29.700
In general,

40
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:34.000
you could,
switch the input mode to other modes.

41
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.833
For example, you could say "always".

42
00:02:36.833 --> 00:02:39.933
Then, the region that will always ask
for human

43
00:02:39.933 --> 00:02:43.200
input before it tries to generate
reply on his own.

44
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.600
And these are only the basic setups
for this agent. In general,

45
00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.900
you could also add code
execution configuration or function

46
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.900
execution, and other settings.

47
00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.400
But let's begin with this, simple setup.

48
00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.633
The first thing you could do is to ask
this agent who generates a response

49
00:03:00.633 --> 00:03:03.900
to a question
using the generate reply method.

50
00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.033
Here we call these agents

51
00:03:08.033 --> 00:03:11.700
generate reply function
and give it to messages list.

52
00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.833
The message has a content.

53
00:03:13.833 --> 00:03:17.100
"Tell me a joke" and "role as a user"

54
00:03:17.433 --> 00:03:20.433
and if we run this,

55
00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:23.833
you should be able
to get a reply from the agent.

56
00:03:23.833 --> 00:03:26.133
So the agent says, "Sure, he's a joke for you.

57
00:03:26.133 --> 00:03:28.100
Why did the scarecrow win an award?

58
00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:30.900
Because he was outstanding in his field."

59
00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.900
So this is the most basic, thing
you could do,

60
00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:37.233
by asking a question
and get a reply from the agent.

61
00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:38.833
Now, if you call this function

62
00:03:38.833 --> 00:03:41.400
again, what would happen?

63
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.100
Let's say, we call this generate reply function

64
00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:44.300
again.

65
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:47.300
Now replace the content
with "repeat the joke".

66
00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:48.400
Right.

67
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:51.400
Do we expect the agent to repeat the joke?

68
00:03:51.933 --> 00:03:52.933
Actually, no.

69
00:03:52.933 --> 00:03:54.300
Because, when we call the generate

70
00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:57.700
reply function, it doesn't alter
the internal state of the agent.

71
00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.533
So when we call the generated

72
00:03:59.533 --> 00:04:03.233
function again, it doesn't know
that it already generated a reply before.

73
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:07.500
So, it will be a fresh function,
for generate reply.

74
00:04:07.533 --> 00:04:11.500
So it will generate the new reply,
without knowing that it has, replied it

75
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:12.500
once before.

76
00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:15.400
You could certainly do
this, in the application for generating

77
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:19.700
different replies if you want,
but we want to keep the state

78
00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:23.800
and maintain the state and make it perform
a series of tasks,

79
00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:25.500
we need a different approach.

80
00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:27.100
In the next part,

81
00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:30.533
let's look at how to create a conversation
between multiple agents.

82
00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.300
And we will do a stand-up comedy example.

83
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.000
We want to create an application
where to stand-up

84
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.300
comedians will talk to each other
and make fun of each other.

85
00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:45.900
So the first agent we'll create
is a conversable agent named Cathy.

86
00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:49.033
In this case,
we give it a system message

87
00:04:49.033 --> 00:04:53.000
to let the agent know your name is Cathy
and your stand-up comedian.

88
00:04:53.333 --> 00:04:57.933
And we'll pass the same, configuration
and the same human input mode to never.

89
00:04:58.433 --> 00:04:59.033
When you don't

90
00:04:59.033 --> 00:05:02.900
specify this system message,
then the agent will have an empty message.

91
00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:06.433
And we'll just perform
as a generic purpose assistant agent.

92
00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:10.800
And using this message,
we customize the behavior of the agent.

93
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.133
Okay, that's one comedian we created.

94
00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:16.600
How about adding another one?

95
00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:21.100
Let's create another conversable agent named Joe
and give the system a message.

96
00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:26.100
"Your name is Joe and your stand-up
comedian", and we add another, instruction,

97
00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:30.500
after that, we say "Start the next joke
from the punchline of the previous joke."

98
00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:31.733
So this gives

99
00:05:31.733 --> 00:05:35.700
the more specific instruction
about how to carry over the conversation.

100
00:05:36.033 --> 00:05:39.100
Okay, so we have two comedians. Now

101
00:05:39.133 --> 00:05:42.800
it's ready to put them to work
and create a conversation.

102
00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:46.033
The way we initiate the conversation is

103
00:05:46.033 --> 00:05:49.833
we will call this initiate
a chat function from one of the agents.

104
00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.333
For example, if we want Joe to start
the conversation,

105
00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.600
we'll call Joe's, initiate
chat function.

106
00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:00.800
We'll set the recipient as Cathy
and give it the initial message.

107
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:03.333
The message says, "I'm Joe. Cathy.

108
00:06:03.333 --> 00:06:05.500
Let's keep the jokes rolling."

109
00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:07.900
We set the max turns to be two.

110
00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:11.500
So, have two turns
of the conversations and then finish.

111
00:06:11.900 --> 00:06:12.800
Let's see what happens.

112
00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:15.200
So

113
00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:19.100
the first message is the same message
as we set here.

114
00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:20.800
"I'm Joe. Cathy,

115
00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.700
Let's keep the jokes rolling."

116
00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:26.000
And next
the message is from Cathy as she said,

117
00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:29.300
"Hey Joe, great to meet another comedy
enthusiast.

118
00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:32.333
let's dive right in with some jokes.

119
00:06:32.333 --> 00:06:35.200
Why did the math book look sad?

120
00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:37.900
Because it had too many problems."

121
00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:40.600
And next turn, Joel says, "Well, Cathy,

122
00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:44.200
at least now we know
why the mask book was always so negative".

123
00:06:44.733 --> 00:06:49.333
So you can see that Joe follows
our previous instruction and starts

124
00:06:49.333 --> 00:06:52.333
the next joke from the last punch line,

125
00:06:52.533 --> 00:06:55.000
and Cathy follows the opposite.

126
00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:55.500
"Exactly.

127
00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:58.500
I just couldn't
subtract the sadness from its pages."

128
00:06:58.733 --> 00:07:01.733
So it's making a continuation
of that joke.

129
00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:04.800
And finally proposes another joke.

130
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:09.200
And after these two turns of exchange,
the conversation stopped.

131
00:07:10.033 --> 00:07:12.300
So after the conversation finishes,

132
00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:15.300
we could inspect the chat history
in the chat result.

133
00:07:15.633 --> 00:07:20.633
So import pprint library
and do a print of the chat history.

134
00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:24.400
So you can see
all the messages that were exchanged

135
00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:28.233
first from Joe, second
from Cathy, and third from Joe.

136
00:07:28.233 --> 00:07:29.933
Cathy again.

137
00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:32.400
And you could also

138
00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:35.433
inspect the token usage in the chat
result.

139
00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:39.500
We'll call the chat result cost function.

140
00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:43.300
We'll see that,
we are using the cheapest GPT-3.5 turbo model.

141
00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:47.533
We consumed 97 completion tokens

142
00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:51.000
and 219 prompt tokens.

143
00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:54.100
And the total tokens is 316.

144
00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:57.100
And the code cost is this much dollars.

145
00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:01.300
So in general, we could define
the conversation in different ways.

146
00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:04.300
You could also check
the summary of the chat result

147
00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:07.600
by calling the chat result
summary function.

148
00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:12.500
So by default, we're using the last message
as the summary of the chat result.

149
00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:17.500
So in this case we see lots and lots of
messages from the conversation by Cathy.

150
00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:20.000
If you want to change the summary method

151
00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:23.000
you could figure
with a different summary method.

152
00:08:23.133 --> 00:08:25.800
For example,
We could run the conversation again

153
00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:29.400
with a different summary method
called reflection with a large language model.

154
00:08:29.633 --> 00:08:33.433
And you can give a summary prompt called
Summarize the Conversation.

155
00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:37.100
So what happens
is after the conversation finishes,

156
00:08:37.300 --> 00:08:40.600
we will call the large language model
with this prompt.

157
00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:45.700
And the large language model will reflect on the
conversation and produce a new summary.

158
00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:49.700
So the same conversation happens
and the same result happens.

159
00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:53.133
Because we're by default
we are using caching to generate

160
00:08:53.133 --> 00:08:56.133
the same messages for the same input.

161
00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:58.300
So now if we check the summary again

162
00:08:59.533 --> 00:09:02.500
we see this time the summary becomes

163
00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:06.400
"the conversation was focused on sharing
jokes and paths between Joe and Cathy.

164
00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:10.000
They playfully exchange
the math and character

165
00:09:10.033 --> 00:09:13.033
related jokes to keep the laughs flowing."

166
00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:16.000
So that's a better summary.

167
00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:19.900
You'll notice that I used
the max terms equal to two to control

168
00:09:19.933 --> 00:09:22.933
how many turns happen
in this conversation.

169
00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:28.500
What if you don't know the right number
of turns before the conversation finishes?

170
00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:29.600
What can you do?

171
00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:32.600
We could change the termination condition

172
00:09:32.633 --> 00:09:36.633
by providing additional, configuration
called "is termination message."

173
00:09:37.100 --> 00:09:38.500
This is a Boolean function.

174
00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:43.500
So it takes a message as input and returns
a true or false. Meaning

175
00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:48.500
whether the message means
and the conversation should be terminated.

176
00:09:48.733 --> 00:09:49.000
Okay.

177
00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:52.400
For example, you will notice that
I changed the system message here.

178
00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:56.100
"So when you are ready
to end the conversation, say, I gotta go."

179
00:09:56.333 --> 00:09:59.333
We also pass this stopping condition

180
00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:03.533
as checking whether the
"I gotta go" is inside of the message.

181
00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:06.633
If we detected that "I gotta go" for this,

182
00:10:06.633 --> 00:10:09.633
we will consider the
conversation, as finished.

183
00:10:09.633 --> 00:10:11.900
And that is given to each agent.

184
00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:12.600
So each agent

185
00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:16.400
will check the condition, from the received
message from the other agent.

186
00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:20.500
And if they see the
"I gotta go" is contained in the message

187
00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:22.600
they received, they will stop replying.

188
00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:26.800
Let's run that and

189
00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:30.400
that's initialize the conversation again

190
00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:34.400
with the new stopping condition
and see what happens.

191
00:10:35.333 --> 00:10:39.300
So the first few messages are similar,

192
00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:44.200
but this time you can see
they have more turns of conversation.

193
00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:49.333
Cathy made a joke, and Joe responded

194
00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:54.633
and Cathy asked Joe
about a different joke, and Joe,

195
00:10:55.133 --> 00:10:58.000
responded with other jokes.

196
00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:02.700
And eventually the last message from
Joe is "Glad you enjoyed it, Cathy.

197
00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:04.700
Puns are always a hit.
Thanks for the laughs.

198
00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:06.233
I gotta go."

199
00:11:06.233 --> 00:11:06.533
Yeah.

200
00:11:06.533 --> 00:11:11.300
So Joe ended the conversation with
"I gotta go" and Cathy sees that phrase.

201
00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:13.633
So it stopped. replying.

202
00:11:13.633 --> 00:11:17.600
So this is a different way of stopping
the conversation and is more flexible.

203
00:11:18.633 --> 00:11:21.100
So after the conversation finishes,

204
00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:24.500
what if you, want to, like,
continue the conversation?

205
00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:28.900
Or if you want to, see whether this time,
the agent can preserve the state.

206
00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:30.733
we can give it give it out the test.

207
00:11:31.733 --> 00:11:32.633
It could test

208
00:11:32.633 --> 00:11:35.633
with the same similar questions
we said before.

209
00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:40.000
Next time, we will
let Cathy send another message.

210
00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:44.333
"What's the last joke we talked about"
and set the recipient to be Joe.

211
00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:48.300
Okay, well, this time, will
they remember

212
00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:50.100
what's the last joke?

213
00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:52.000
Let's check that.

214
00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.100
Bingo. Joe responded.

215
00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:54.800
"The last joke we talked

216
00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:57.900
about was the Scarecrow winning award
because he was outstanding.

217
00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:02.900
in his field". And they also follow
the same termination condition.

218
00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:07.233
And, so we see this time Cathy said,
"I gotta go" So Joe also knew

219
00:12:07.533 --> 00:12:09.900
that's a signal
of stopping the conversation.

220
00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:11.700
They stop replying.

221
00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:16.700
So this demonstrated a way
to make agents work in conversations.

222
00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:19.533
Start a conversation, continue
the conversation.

223
00:12:19.533 --> 00:12:21.433
Remember where it hit.

224
00:12:21.433 --> 00:12:25.033
And this is just a very basic,
demonstration of how

225
00:12:25.033 --> 00:12:28.600
to use the conversable agent to construct
a conversation between two agents.

226
00:12:28.833 --> 00:12:33.500
In the next few lessons, we will learn
many other conversation patterns

227
00:12:33.833 --> 00:12:37.833
and some agent design patterns,
including tool using

228
00:12:38.033 --> 00:12:42.400
reflection,
planning and code execution, etc...

